WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.490
Model selection begins with a job statement, not a leaderboard.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.930
Describe the input, required output, latency, volume, privacy boundary, error cost,

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.100
and reviewer.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.780
A model cannot be judged useful until success is tied to an operational task.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.690
CCTV event review may need concise descriptions and reliable identification of visible

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.685
activity.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:22.440
Document and screenshot analysis may depend on small-text OCR and layout.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.555
Product inspection may require consistent recognition of a narrow defect under controlled

00:00:26.555 --> 00:00:27.244
lighting.

00:00:27.244 --> 00:00:30.810
Charts and diagrams combine perception with reasoning about labels, relationships,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.365
and values.

00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:35.120
Home-lab experimentation may favor broad capability and easy setup.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.055
Security alerts require conservative output, visible evidence,

00:00:38.055 --> 00:00:40.680
and human confirmation before consequential action.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.715
Batch review can tolerate slower inference if throughput and completion are stable.

00:00:44.715 --> 00:00:47.960
Near-real-time monitoring has stricter latency and concurrency needs.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.290
A model that answers one image well may not keep pace with several streams or a large

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.080
queue.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.590
Define representative normal cases, difficult cases, and costly failures.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:59.430
For OCR, specify character accuracy and formatting.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.245
For descriptions,

00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:04.120
define which facts must be present and which unsupported claims are unacceptable.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.800
For counting, state the expected range and occlusion conditions.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.576
No universal best model exists because use cases value different tradeoffs.

00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:12.725
Shortlist models that fit the task and hardware,

00:01:12.725 --> 00:01:16.000
then evaluate them locally with the same prompts, runtime conditions,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.360
and human-scored criteria.
